    "body": "> The explainer only touches on this briefly and somewhat vaguely, but I think there is a key issue that needs to be explored.\n> \n> GIFs are not built for streaming.\n> \n> This means that to seek to the middle or end of a GIF, which is trivial and can even be accidental in the usual video UI or API, the browser would have to download and decode all of the frames up to that point one by one.\n\nIsn't this also the case for all existing ways to display images? E.g. `<img>` etc. Or do you mean this would happen anew **on every seeking action**?\n\n> This means that an accidental click or js call can cause a multi MB download and a well meaning web developer might not even know they're causing that.\n> \n> The explainer does show some alternative UI affordances that don't support seeking, which seems promising, and we should consider if this should affect what seeking APIs can do.\n\nIndeed, ideally the desired UI for the vast majority of these use cases is much simpler than the current `<video controls>`. But also, there are bonafide video and audio use cases that need simpler UI and currently have to build it from scratch, which highlights the importance of having more control over which controls are shown (though I don't want to stir up the `controlslist` controversy…).",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
