    "body": "> > The explainer only touches on this briefly and somewhat vaguely, but I think there is a key issue that needs to be explored.\n> > GIFs are not built for streaming.\n> > This means that to seek to the middle or end of a GIF, which is trivial and can even be accidental in the usual video UI or API, the browser would have to download and decode all of the frames up to that point one by one.\n> \n> Isn't this also the case for all existing ways to display images? E.g. `<img>` etc. Or do you mean this would happen anew **on every seeking action**?\n\nCurrently animated images can't be \"fast forwarded\" or \"seeked\", each frame is presented in sequence. so a 5-minute GIF would take 5 minute to run and the operation would be visible. This is different from seeking to the middle of a very long GIF.\n\nBut perhaps this issue means something simple-ish like playing with the \"seekable\" regions of an animated image and not letting the user jump ahead, kind of like live-stream videos/WebRTC (or a variation on this). \n\nNote: despite these gotchas I am so far convinced by the explainer that img-in-video is the best approach and better than developers doing this themselves in JS.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
