    "body": "> This was discussed in [#11622](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11622) today, see minutes in that issue. (This was an unplanned addition at the end of the meeting, apologies that it wasn't announced ahead of time.)\n\nBummer, I missed this one due to a conflict. Didn't expect this issue to be discussed or I'd have tried to attend both meetings.\n\nIt feels like some of the discussion may be tackling a slightly different question. The framing here isn’t “does this solve everything?” but rather “is this a good step forward?” This proposal was never intended as a complete solution, but as a way to make things possible while we work on better solutions. Currently, there is absolutely *no* way to control playback of animated images, and any solution that involves new API surface will take a lot longer. Since from an end-user perspective, animated images don’t differ conceptually from a video without audio (despite the underlying technical differences), this seems like meaningful progress — even if it doesn’t fully solve the problem on its own.\n\nThe fact that user-generated content editors produce `<img>`, which would need to be transformed to `<video>` is an issue @frivoal and I are well aware of. Same with `(prefers-reduced-motion)`. I believe both are mentioned in the explainer.\n\nWe've explored many other solutions for these remaining bits, some of which we presented to the CSS WG in the August F2F. \n[Minutes are here](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/1615#issuecomment-3210179592) (literally one comment above @keithamus's advocating for exactly this approach 😅) and [slides are here](https://webplatform.design/talks/image-animation/). As discussed there, we do not see these solutions as mutually exclusive, but as complementary.",
